Oxbow Philharmonia
Ingredients: SOUTHERN ROCK + CINEMATIC, EPIC & ORCHESTRAL + Guitar
Creative Catalyst: Handcrafted slide guitars and resonators forged with celestial meteorite nickel-iron and antique clockwork gear assemblies.

Oxbow Philharmonia is a colossal, dust-caked collision of swampy Southern rock swagger and towering cinematic symphonism. Searing bottleneck slide guitars and harmonized twin-lead Les Pauls ride upon cinematic brass swells, sweeping orchestral string sections, and the polyrhythmic mechanical ticking of geared escapements. It sounds like an unreleased 1970s Muscle Shoals tracking session conducted by a 60-piece philharmonic orchestra inside an abandoned cathedral of rust and timber.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 68–84 BPM. A slow, heavy southern backbeat featuring dragging half-time shuffles, syncopated orchestral timpani impacts, and clockwork escapement micro-rhythms.
- Harmonic Palette: Mixolydian and Dorian blues-rock riffs intertwined with dramatic Aeolian film-score modulations, open-G slide tunings, and lush, cinematic major-sixth string voicings.
- Production Signatures: "Escapement Foley" (contact mics capturing antique clock gears ticking rhythmically inside hollow guitar bodies); "Tall-Timber Convolution" (warm analog tape saturation coupled with massive, hall-sized orchestral reverbs); and octave unisons pairing overdriven guitar leads directly with French horns and cellos.
- Dynamics: Starts as an intimate, grease-stained porch blues with lonely slide guitar, slowly gathers momentum as low woodwinds and cellos creep in, and detonates into an earth-shattering fortissimo crescendo of dual guitars, blaring brass, and booming concert bass drums before decaying into ticking silence.

Scene & Origin Lore
The genre coalesced in the foothills of Lookout Mountain, Alabama, when a collective of classically trained conservancy dropouts and third-generation luthier-mechanics took over a shuttered 19th-century textile mill. Salvaging charred nickel-iron from an 1880s bolide impact site alongside dismantled municipal tower clocks, they fashioned one-of-a-kind bridges, slides, and mechanical B-benders that ticked with astronomical escapement precision. The scene flourished in gravel-pit amphitheatres and open-air sawmill clearings, drawing a subculture known as "The Clock-Gaugers"—people dressed in grease-stained chore jackets, heavy wool dusters, and formal formalwear pinned with antique watch chains. Rituals involved listeners tuning portable shortwave receivers to pick up sub-harmonic radio bleed from the band's massive tube amplifiers while a full chamber orchestra shared folding chairs with sweat-soaked rock drummers under the southern summer humidity.

Typical Instruments Used: Nickel-meteorite resonator guitars, clockwork B-bender electric guitars, vintage tube-driven half-stacks, 30-piece string orchestra, French horn and trombone choir, concert timpani, upright piano, Hammond B3 organ.

For Fans Of: The Allman Brothers Band, Ennio Morricone, Godspeed You! Black Emperor, Drive-By Truckers

